<p><strong>Product:</strong> <a href="https://kosshi.app/" target="_blank">Kosshi - macOS/iOS Outliner</a><br>
<strong>Price</strong>: $24.99 from <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/kosshi-fast-outliner/id6759483880" target="_blank">App Store</a></p>
<p><strong>Kosshi</strong> is a new outliner for macOS and iOS. I am going to cover the macOS version.</p>
<p>What does Kosshi mean? I asked the developer, Junichi Sato, and he said that the Japanese use the word “outline” to describe this kind of program. The native word for that concept is 骨子 (kosshi), probably Chinese in origin. The characters literally mean “the bones” of something, and the word is used for the outline, the gist, or the framework of an idea. That’s the angle the developer picked for the name.</p>
<p>Kosshi is different from every outliner I have covered for the blog. It is a database-backed outlining application.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/e0b8ec53-d3dd-470f-9119-c92c7388f305/kosshi01.png" alt="One File" width="1368" height="1273" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">One File</span></p>
<p>Let me say that again. It is one database file. Kosshi doesn’t let you create a different file. You do not have the ability to have different files for different outlines. Everything you write in Kosshi is in the same one-file. Everything.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/f19ea18c-38c8-48e8-884c-9d5ac694d94c/kosshi02.png" alt="Separate Window" width="1381" height="1046" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">Separate Window</span></p>
<p>You have the ability to open a section in a new window (<code>⌘+n</code>), like this. And write in it. Gives you a view into your file where the section that you are dealing with is in focus and everything else is tucked away in the sidebar. It is analogous to the hoist function in most other outlining programs.</p>
<p>But it is one file. Let that sink in for a while. The developer says that Kosshi can handle a million rows. I haven’t tested that claim. But that is the assertion. One file. Sections for all of your writing in outline form. A million rows.</p>
<p>Bizarre was a word which came to mind on my first exposure to Kosshi. Then I explored the preferences of the program, primarily looking for a way around the one-file restriction.</p>
<h2 id="kosshi-preferences">Kosshi Preferences</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/cfce5a30-408f-46bc-b1a6-4645ec3e91c8/kosshi03.png" alt="General" width="612" height="663" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">General</span></p>
<p>Kosshi ships with a light and dark mode alternative. You get to choose your own fonts for regular text and code. You get to define the font size, the line height, and the row spacing. You get to select the size of the font in the sidebar. Kosshi supports Markdown, so you have the ability to hide/show the Markdown syntax. It has the ability to show indent guides. Kosshi supports tags and nested tags, so you can show those tags in the sidebar.<br>
I am impressed. This is the list of things I want to be able to customize in my outliner. The preference pane gave me everything I needed.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/fa7a926a-e9a1-42df-897d-94337aadb99e/kosshi04.png" alt="Writing" width="612" height="317" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">Writing</span></p>
<p>Spell Check, Smart Quotes and Smart Dashes.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/424c7541-81d7-4f9c-b1eb-0ab6e9bbac25/kosshi05.png" alt="Sync" width="612" height="384" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">Sync</span></p>
<p>Kosshi syncs with iCloud. So you have the same document in your iPad and iPhone.</p>
<p>Koshi also has the ability to sync your dated tags to the macOS Reminders or Calendar Application. Excuse me, what? I will revisit this later in the review.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/63c8408f-f3ce-4d64-8385-6f78b7ac2c52/kosshi06.png" alt="Backups" width="612" height="522" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">Backups</span></p>
<p>Kosshi auto-backups your document. The backups are there forever, or 30 days, 90 days or a year, depending on your preference.</p>
<p>The preferences are well-designed and they are adequate. I can’t think of anything that I would add to the mix. If a developer spent this kind of effort on the design and implementation of the preference panels, the program might be worth looking into.</p>
<p>I decided to dampen my initial reaction and delve deeper based on my reaction to the preference panels.</p>
<h2 id="understanding-the-one-document-structure">Understanding The One-Document Structure</h2>
<p>The developer of Kosshi makes an argument for the single outline <a href="https://kosshi.app/one-outline" target="_blank">Everything in One Outline — Why a File-Free Design Works - Kosshi</a>.</p>
<p>The argument boils down to the task of reducing friction by reducing the number of decisions that an user has to make in the process of writing. It is also an argument for the wisdom of tagging and searching.</p>
<p>The inspiration behind this decision rests on three prior products: <a href="https://workflowy.com" target="_blank">Workflowy</a>, <a href="https://scrapbox.io" target="_blank">Cosense</a> and <a href="https://bear.app" target="_blank">Bear</a>. I had not heard of Cosense. I have tried both Workflowy and Bear. The organization task in Bear is based on tags and nested tags. So I understand the implementation in Kosshi. Workflowy does have the similar one outline for everything structure that Kosshi features.</p>
<p>I found it easier to get my head around Kosshi and the one document focus by realizing that this is a database. Every outline in it is a node in the database. You go to an outline (node) put it into a new window, write. Close the window. You are done. It is more similar to Bear than it is to OmniOutliner. Once I got that into my head, I didn’t find it bizarre anymore. In fact, I started loving the implementation.</p>
<h3 id="what-are-the-advantages-of-this-decision">What Are the Advantages of This Decision?</h3>
<ul>
<li>You don’t have to worry about files and folders. You open Kosshi, go to the section that you think is relevant, and start writing. If you don’t have a relevant section, start a new one. Tag the content with multiple tags, nested or otherwise and that is your organization schema.</li>
<li>The Sidebar and the Tags are all you need for your organization of content and you are not dealing with the file-system at all. That is an advantage in that it does reduce the number of decisions you are making before writing anything.</li>
</ul>
<p>Being used to the one file per project world, it stuck me as weird at first, but the more I use Kosshi, the more comfortable I get with it. It makes sense.</p>
<h2 id="markdown-support">Markdown Support</h2>
<p>Kosshi supports Markdown. It supports bold, italic, strikethrough, inline code, links, checkboxes, block quotes, and code blocks. It doesn’t support headings. The structure of the outline defines the headings and the user does not need to provide that. It is provided by Kosshi on export. The program also supports ordered and unordered lists in Markdown. It auto-continues lists. You start the note with a <code>-</code> or a number and you have an unordered or an ordered list.</p>
<h2 id="task-management">Task Management</h2>
<p>Kosshi does task management. You can sync your tasks to the Reminders app, or the Calendar app on macOS. You get to choose where in the Preferences.</p>
<p>Any <code>@tag</code> with a date in parentheses syncs to Apple Reminders or Calendar. “The tag name can be anything. As long as the parentheses contain a date, it syncs.” Example, <code>@todo(2026-05-25)</code>, or <code>@report(every Monday 9:00)</code>, for single tasks or repeated tasks.</p>
<p>For task lists type <code>[]</code> and it becomes a task. You can have a list of tasks. You complete a task and hit <strong>⌘↵</strong> to mark it as completed. To explore this in detail, read <a href="https://kosshi.app/todo-reminder" target="_blank">Task Management with an Outliner</a>.</p>
<h2 id="images-and-files">Images and Files</h2>
<p>You can paste or drag to add images. Resize them. PDFs and videos preview inline too. Makes Kosshi super useful.</p>
<h2 id="internal-links">Internal Links</h2>
<p>Use the usual wikilinks format to link to different rows. Start with <code>[[</code> and the program gives you a drop-down completion of sections you can link to.</p>
<h2 id="bookmarks">Bookmarks</h2>
<p>You can bookmark a section in the outline. It shows up in a special section in the sidebar. This function is a little buggy. Doesn’t work reliably, but it is an useful feature when you are working in multiple sections of the outline and you need to move between them easily.</p>
<p>It also helps keep sections you are working on easy to reach. This becomes useful the more sections you have in Kosshi.</p>
<h2 id="backups">Backups</h2>
<p>One of the things I worried about when dealing with a database for my outlines is data-integrity. Kosshi’s data is stored locally as a SQLite database. There is no dependence on an external server. Your computer contains the data and there is no one else who has access to it. The program has an auto-backup function and it can keep your backups for as long as you want. It also has a manual backup function where it backs up all the outline rows (text, structure, metadata) and images and file attachments into a zip file to your chosen destination.</p>
<h2 id="export">Export</h2>
<p>You can export the contents of Kosshi (<code>⇧⌘E</code>) to markdown, plain text, or OPML. It is easy and it works well.</p>
<h2 id="adapting-kosshi-through-keyboard-maestro">Adapting Kosshi Through Keyboard Maestro</h2>
<p>I have been using outlining programs for a long time and have some muscle memory for some common tasks. I wanted to have the same keyboard commands in Kosshi. Since Kosshi doesn’t let you change the keyboard command assignments, these are the changes I made through Keyboard Maestro.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/9f81339c-5edb-4fe1-9bed-5ba2c36c17a8/kkm01.png" alt="Add Inside" width="449" height="330" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">Add Inside</span></p>
<p>Press <code>⌥⌘i</code> to add a child row.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/67bb63ff-c6cf-4b09-9897-b6cecc337686/kkm02.png" alt="Add Outside" width="431" height="333" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">Add Outside</span></p>
<p>Press <code>⌥⌘o</code> to add a parent row.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/0b546c58-d463-47ff-8320-85a4a7e3972d/kkm03.png" alt="Move Row Down" width="416" height="252" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">Move Row Down</span></p>
<p>Press ⌃⌘↓ to move the row down.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/da9484b7-f0c4-4d75-8446-772f530790d8/kkm04.png" alt="Move Row Up" width="413" height="275" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">Move Row Up</span></p>
<p>Press ⌃⌘↑ to move the row up.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/c75914af-485d-45a0-af78-a031dcf4a229/kkm05.png" alt="Move Row Left" width="413" height="266" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">Move Row Left</span></p>
<p>Press ⌃⌘← to move row left, or promote a row.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/efd41477-32bd-469e-b72e-41ca9dc08ffb/kkm06.png" alt="Move Row Right" width="424" height="259" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">Move Row Right</span></p>
<p>Press ⌃⌘→ to move row right or demote a row.</p>
<p>These might not be useful to anyone else, but these are useful to me and showing you this lets me tout the usefulness of <a href="https://www.keyboardmaestro.com/" target="_blank">Keyboard Maestro</a>. I can now use the keyboard commands which are built into my muscle memory in Kosshi.</p>
<h2 id="suggested-improvements">Suggested Improvements</h2>
<ul>
<li>Would love the ability to have a section with a description and then children within it with tasks. The parent would show how many tasks there are within a task list and how many have been completed.</li>
<li>The bookmarking function is a little buggy. Sometimes it works and sometimes you have to press the keyboard command several times to get it to take.</li>
<li>A way to customize the keyboard commands would help.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
<p>Kosshi has been a revelation. Well thought out, well designed software, it is an absolute joy to live in and use. The rationale behind the product, the implementation of the feature set, the thought that has gone into every design decision is awe-inspiring.</p>
